From: ThaRae <76752281+ThaRae@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 29 Oct 2026 08:52:27 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] LibWebView: Capture PageInfoType by value in delayed text
 dumps

--screenshot-delay starts a one-shot timer with a [&] lambda. type is a
by-value parameter, so that capture is a dangling reference after the
frame returns. WebContent then built whichever PageInfoType bits later
overwrote the stack slot, and headless text dumps mixed inner text with
the layout tree, stacking-context tree, display list, accessibility tree
and GC graph (22 MiB instead of a few hundred bytes). Capture type by
value so delayed dumps stay PageInfoType::Text.
---
 Libraries/LibWebView/Application.cpp | 5 ++++-
 1 file changed, 4 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/Libraries/LibWebView/Application.cpp b/Libraries/LibWebView/Application.cpp
index 465918e89f425d274cdf9db9c3b7980cc5c257c4..19f4f1b49c6ef1ab44e6f26422573beffeb8a37f 100644
--- a/Libraries/LibWebView/Application.cpp
+++ b/Libraries/LibWebView/Application.cpp
@@ -2358,9 +2358,12 @@ static NonnullRefPtr<Core::Timer> load_page_for_info_after_delay_and_exit(Core::
 {
     outln("Dumping page text after {} seconds", delay_seconds);
 
+    // `type` is a by-value parameter, so capture it by value: this callback runs long after this frame is gone, and a
+    // reference to the parameter read whatever later code had written over that stack slot. That turned text dumps
+    // into text plus the layout tree, stacking-context tree, display list, accessibility tree and GC graph.
     auto timer = Core::Timer::create_single_shot(
         AK::Duration::from_seconds(delay_seconds),
-        [&]() {
+        [&event_loop, &view, type]() {
             request_page_info_and_quit(event_loop, view, type);
         });
 
